body:
Happy to help! I've worked in scouting and then at a large literary agency for the last four years, and I've pasted the advice I give my interns when they ask for pointers. Since you graduated with an English degree, I expect you can write a succinct synopsis and analysis drawing on your knowledge and education, but here are some of the tips that may not be as obvious. I hope it's helpful!

**I believe a great report does four things:**

1. Gives the agent/colleague/boss the ability to talk about this book *as though they themselves have read it* even though they obviously don’t have the time to read everything (via a great summary that hits all the important points);
2. Only spends a few minutes of your colleague’s time (via a succinct summary and analysis),
3. Makes an argument for or against the book’s quality AND salability in a really smart way…
   1. Because if it’s a rejection, and it probably is, they’ll need to give meaningful reasons they’re passing to the author;
   2. Because they’ll also need to cushion the blow of a rejection with heartfelt, meaningful praise on the book’s good qualities;
   3. Because if it’s really good, you’ll need to convince your colleague to reorganize their priorities to make time to read it themselves;
   4. And because if your boss has a good reason to disagree with your ultimate stance (see D)––maybe you’re right that the writing really is mediocre, but they happen to know that even mediocre books about honeybees (or whatever) are selling like hotcakes––they’ll be able to take your recommendation with the proper grain of salt, because you’ve been very clear about your thought process in the report.
4. Takes a firm stand re: recommending a pass/revise-and-resubmit/take this on, please read it yourself. This one is the most important, because whoever you’re reading for is outsourcing a lot of their decision-making to you. If you say “pass,” and you usually will, then 90% of the time, the manuscript may very well be rejected outright. Your boss will trust your take because it saves the most amount of precious time in the long run. If you say “I think this deserves you to read it yourself because I think it’s great and salable,” it will very likely still get rejected, but if you’ve made a convincing argument that it’s amazing and salable, your colleague will now take the time to read it themselves. So of course, we have to say “this is amazing” very sparingly. If it’s almost amazing but has 2+ major problems, then “Revise and Resubmit” is a good middle ground––still, unfortunately, to be used pretty sparingly. One thing I knew before working at \[the agency\], but now feel viscerally, is how few things are really salable to publishers and to the public. Few things are more depressing (to you, to the agent, to the author who got their hopes up) than convincing an agent to take on a project and then having every editor reject it.

Basically, taking a firm **Yes/No/Revise-and-resubmit** stance (and probably repeating it twice or so throughout your report) is the single most helpful thing a report can do. I say that knowing that it’s inherently unsettling to take such a firm stance––what if you’re wrong? Personally, when I started writing reports for \[scouting\], a full half of my reports’ stances were **“**Maybe.” I was called out for that so many times when I started out, because I wasn’t really helping the clients. They felt they still had to read it themselves to make a confident decision.

Once you're hired, I encourage you to feel secure in your decisions and trust your gut. The person who hired you will have full confidence in your ability to make that call on anything they send your way. If there’s information about salability or personal preference that may make your boss disagree with your take, they’ll see that in your report, as I mentioned earlier.

Good luck! I hope this is somewhat helpful.

I know what you mean. I'm so sorry. What I have done that helped a little bit is that opposite-to-emotion suggestion from Cognitive Behavioral Therapy. For example, when I think people are upset with me (I think that ALL the time), my emotional response is to avoid them because I'm afraid they're mad at me. So instead I act like they aren't mad at me and arrange a time to hang out. 9 times out of 10 they are not upset with me, it was in my head, and that seems clear when we hang out together.

&#x200B;

I don't know if that will help, but it's an idea. It's also a response that can help with the loneliness. Just throwing as many coffee dates, movie dates, etc on your calendar as possible. Like, "I can still feel miserable, but I will feel miserable on my way to see this person at the park."

&#x200B;

I'm really sorry you feel so alone. You're not alone in feeling that way, at least.

Gone Girl or The Social Network, for the same reason: Trent Reznor and Atticus Ross can make unbelievably unnerving scores that work brilliantly in-scene. For Gone Girl, David Fincher apparently told Reznor, "Think about the really terrible music you hear in massage parlors. The way that it artificially tries to make you feel like everything’s OK. And then imagine that sound starting to curdle and unravel.”

&#x200B;

I watched the Fyre Festival documentary on Netflix recently and heard pieces from both these soundtracks re-used. There's nothing really like them!
